{# A page that is prose rather than rows.

   Two families use it. /about is the repository README rendered; /help is five
   articles somebody wrote. They were built far apart and looked it -- the
   README page in Bootstrap's `display-4` and `.lead` straight on the grey
   background, the help pages on a white sheet with an icon and a measure. The
   same kind of page twice, with nothing shared above them but the layout.

   Embedded rather than included, because a page has to be able to fill `body`
   with whatever it has: /about holds already-safe HTML in a variable and an
   article has a template of its own, and a block inside a plain `{% include %}`
   cannot be overridden by the page including it. Same reason the search
   sidebar's sections are embedded.

   `note` is for a line that belongs to the document -- where the README came
   from -- and `aside` for a panel beside it, which is where an article puts its
   siblings. Both are empty unless a page fills them. #}
<main role="main" class="article">
    <div class="container article__layout">
        <div class="article__sheet">
            <h1 class="article__title">
                <i class="fas {{ icon }} article__title-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                {{ title }}
            </h1>

            {# The sentence the page is about, set apart from the answer rather
               than run into it. On an article it is also the meta description,
               so somebody arriving from a search result reads the sentence that
               brought them. #}
            {% if lead is defined and lead is not empty %}
                <p class="article__lead">{{ lead }}</p>
            {% endif %}

            {# `body_class` is for prose nobody here wrote. The README arrives as
               generic markdown with no classes to hook, so `.readme` carries
               the levels and the blocks only it has -- code, tables, an image.
               An article needs none of that and passes nothing. #}
            <div class="article__body{{ body_class is defined and body_class ? ' ' ~ body_class : '' }}">
                {% block body %}{% endblock %}
            </div>

            {% block note %}{% endblock %}
        </div>

        {% block aside %}{% endblock %}
    </div>
</main>
